A||||4||overlord||wet engine?||||1||
Z||000000||overlord||12-20-1999||08:45 AM||||a couple days ago i had todrive home through our first blizzard.  (yes this is my first time with my fiero in the winter), and it handled great on the road but just before i got home i noticed i had no low end power. almost like running on 2 cyls. (4 cylinder)<BR>I am guessing because the fiero is so low to the ground, snow swirls up into the engine and get things wet.<P>is there something i can do to help keep the vitals dry?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-20-1999||01:34 PM||||Ah...Finally a question I can actually answer.  The same thing happened to me after I washed my car for the 1st time.  I got too much water on my motor.  I lifted the hood and heard some popping sounds, too.  It was suggested that I might have gotten some water in some of the electrical connections.  After the motor dried out, I checked the connections, put some liquid electrical tape on the ones in question, and washed my motor again.  Ran fine and no popping.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||12-20-1999||06:35 PM||||try cleaning the plug wires, distributor cap, rotor, and coil. Dirt on these will draw water and short the secondary ignition loop.<P>If there is any damage at all replace plug wires cap and rotor if it's a distributor motor.||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||12-20-1999||08:38 PM||||spraying the cap and wires with hair spray helps keep things dry||||reg||1
Z||000004||DJRice||12-21-1999||08:53 AM||||IF you plan on washing your engine regularly, it cant hurt to pull the distributor cap and put a small bead of sealer around the bottom and then reinstall.<P>I drove my car through a puddle one day during a terrential down pour and the whole thing shut down on me.  I had to let it sit for a couple of hours and she fired right back up.  I never did figure out what caused that, but I never had the problem again either.||||reg||1
